<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Shah II of Johor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1675">1675</a>–<a href="/years/1699">1699</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Shah II was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> from 1685 to 1699 and the last ruler descended from the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. He came to the throne as a child, and his reign was remembered for its cruelty. In 1699 he was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-tinggi">Kota Tinggi</a> by the nobleman Megat Sri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, whose pregnant wife he had had killed, and the bendahara then took the throne as Abdul Jalil IV.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mahmud Shah II succeeded his father, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah">Ibrahim Shah</a>, in 1685. Ibrahim had extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>'s territory and prosperity until the state held much of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> as well as eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. Because the new sultan was a young child, Johor was governed by a joint regency of his mother and the Bendahara Paduka Raja. Only when the bendahara died on 27 July 1697 did Mahmud take on all the duties of sultan himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>European trading company records and local texts alike describe an erratic ruler of cruel nature and a state they called ungovernable. Local texts tell of violence against women, some ordered to be executed for minor offences. The Scottish country trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> in 1695, recorded the sultan firing a gun into a man in the street to test the weapon, and in his A New Account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> of 1727 described him as preferring men. Traders began to avoid the main port, and the economic damage, together with violence against women of the orang kaya, the Malay nobility, led the high officials to move against him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> reports his body lay exposed at the bendahara's residence until late afternoon and was buried that night with little ceremony; his grave survives in a village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-tinggi">Kota Tinggi</a> still called Kampung Makam, the village of the tomb. Because regicide was unthinkable in Malay culture, legends soon arose about his death, and the name Sultan Mahmud Mangkat Dijulang recalls that he died while carried in a royal litter. The story was retold in court texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuhfat-al-nafis">Tuhfat al-Nafis</a> and the Hikayat Siak, and in the 1961 film Sultan Mahmud Mangkat Dijulang.</p><p>With the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> sultans ended, the Bendahara dynasty struggled for two decades to win support. By 1718 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kecil">Raja Kecil</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, who claimed to be Mahmud's son, conceived on the eve of the murder, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a> support. After four years he withdrew and founded the Siak Sultanate in 1722, and the bendahara's descendants returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> mercenaries, laying the foundation of the Johor-Riau-Lingga sultanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Shah II of Johor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The murder of Sultan <b>Mahmud Shah II</b> in 1699, the last of the Malacca line, broke the sacred bond between ruler and subjects; the bendahara took the throne as Sultan Abdul Jalil IV, but many Malays and the Orang Laut questioned the new dynasty's legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-tinggi">Kota Tinggi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=1.73,103.9&amp;year=1690" aria-label="Kota Tinggi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Mahmud Shah II of Johor"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6177 4852h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6177 4852h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=1.73,103.9&amp;year=1690">What was here in 1690? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
